<!-- ===== SECTION 6: RECOMMENDATIONS ===== -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-number">06 — Strategic Recommendations</div>
    <div class="section-title">Strategic Recommendations</div>
    <div class="section-desc">Actionable decisions derived directly from the data above. Each recommendation references a specific quantitative finding.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-grid">
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-icon sourcing">🎯</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <div class="rec-tag">Sourcing Strategy</div>
        <div class="rec-title">Source Apple from the UK — 28.6% discount to global median</div>
        <p>The United Kingdom lists Apple at a median of <strong>$392.74</strong> versus the global median of <strong>$550</strong> — a 28.6% discount backed by 214 listings. This is the deepest sustained discount in the dataset for any major brand in a high-volume market. Prioritize UK-based Apple sourcing for resale into AU, JP, or IT channels where Apple trades at 9–44% above the global median.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-icon arb">🌏</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <div class="rec-tag">Geographic Arbitrage</div>
        <div class="rec-title">Japan is a consistent buy-market across all three brands</div>
        <p>Japan prices all three brands below the global median: Apple −25.1%, Garmin −10.7%, Samsung −17.5%. With 185 Japan-based listings in the dataset, there is sufficient supply to build a systematic sourcing pipeline. Route Samsung and Apple sourced in Japan into the UK (Samsung +55.7%) and Australia (Apple +44%) for maximum margin capture — the spread exceeds $400 in both cases.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-icon arb">📦</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <div class="rec-tag">Brand Focus</div>
        <div class="rec-title">Garmin is the highest-consistency brand — prioritize for stable margin</div>
        <p>Garmin carries the highest median resale price ($567) and the <strong>lowest price variance</strong> (std dev $198 vs $834 for Apple, $2,286 for Samsung). Unlike Apple, Garmin shows no condition premium — new and used list at nearly identical prices — eliminating condition grading risk. For operators who want predictable margins without model-level expertise, Garmin is the lowest-risk inventory choice.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-icon size">📐</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <div class="rec-tag">Pricing Intelligence</div>
        <div class="rec-title">Do not use case size as a pricing signal — use brand and condition instead</div>
        <p>Case size correlates at just r=0.021 with price across the full dataset. For Apple, case size explains essentially nothing (r=0.033). The only partial exception is Garmin (r=0.107), where the 47–55mm bucket commands a $75 premium. Pricing algorithms should weight <strong>brand, condition, and geography</strong> heavily over physical dimensions. Investing in model-line identification (Series vs Ultra vs SE for Apple) will yield far greater pricing accuracy than measuring case diameter.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-icon seller">🤝</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <div class="rec-tag">Seller Partnerships</div>
        <div class="rec-title">Partner selectively with power sellers — expect to pay 15% above market</div>
        <p>The top 5% of sellers (88 accounts) control 48.3% of listings and price 15% above casual sellers. They are <strong>not competing on price</strong> — they compete on breadth and reliability. The value of a power seller partnership is consistent access to Garmin supply (35% of power seller listings vs 7% for casual). If sourcing Garmin at scale, budget for the 11.7% premium as a supply reliability cost. For Samsung, the 24.7% power seller premium makes casual sourcing far more attractive.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-icon sourcing">💡</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <div class="rec-tag">Condition Grading</div>
        <div class="rec-title">Prioritize "New" condition for Apple; condition is irrelevant for Garmin</div>
        <p>Apple shows a <strong>17.2% new-vs-used premium</strong> ($590 vs $503) — the largest condition premium of the three brands. For Apple inventory, condition grading directly affects realized margin. Garmin and Samsung both show near-zero condition premiums (−0.5% and +1.4% respectively), meaning condition certification costs for those brands will not be recovered in higher prices. Grade Apple aggressively; treat Garmin and Samsung condition as operationally irrelevant for pricing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
